Tiles ahead of a scroll are made in the update that needs them.

The grey or white areas that cover part of the view while a heavy page is
scrolled (amazon.de: the lower half of the view, for a moment, after every
few wheel steps) are what threaded scrolling shows where there are no tiles.
The scrolling thread moves the layers on its own; tiles are created and
recorded only by the main thread, in a rendering update.  So what the page
can be scrolled through without waiting is what the last update covered
ahead of the viewport -- and createOrDestroyTiles() created only the missing
tiles at the shortest distance from the viewport's centre in each update:
the visible ones in one go, then one ring of tile rows per further update,
each one a complete rendering update that waits for the raster of the one
before (TilesPending -> notifySubsequentFlushRequired -> a new update).  With
256-pixel tile rows the cover area ahead grew by 256 pixels per update; any
scroll faster than that drained it, and the view showed the background
until enough updates had run.  Wheel scrolling on the main thread never
showed it because there the layer moves in the same update that creates the
tiles for its new position.

Upstream made the same diagnosis (306119@main: "It scrolls a page even while
the main thread is blocked.  If it scrolled to the outside of coverage area
meanwhile, there were no tiles to paint.") and, for the new Skia compositor
only, creates every tile of the cover area in the layer flush of the visible
ones (325723@main, bug 329076).  2.52.6 has the TextureMapper compositor, in
which a rendering update's frame waits for every tile it carries, so here the
part of the cover area that matters -- the area ahead of the viewport in the
direction the content last scrolled -- counts as visible: it is created,
recorded and painted with the visible tiles, in the same update, and the
other sides are still filled ring by ring.  The cover area reaches a full
viewport further that way (one and a half viewports ahead in all), so that
one update of the main thread covers what the scrolling thread can move
through before the next one.

The dirty tiles of a layer were also recorded as one picture of the bounding
box of all of them.  With rows being created a viewport and a half ahead,
and any animation at the top of the page dirtying a tile there, that box is
most of the cover area, painted on the main thread at every update for
tiles that are clean.  They are now recorded in bands, one per run of
consecutive tile rows with dirty tiles.

Under memory pressure the cover area is the visible rect, as before.

